  "raw": {
    "https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/faculty-positions/#chemistry-assistant-teaching-professor-of-chemistry": {
      "title": "CHEMISTRY: Assistant Teaching Professor of Chemistry",
      "description": "CHEMISTRY: Assistant Teaching Professor of Chemistry. (updated 8/12/26) The Chemistry Department at Hollins University in Roanoke, Virginia invites applications for an assistant teaching professor. This is a full-time, on-campus, non-tenure-track position beginning July 1, 2027. The position is a renewable, three-year appointment, subject to satisfactory performance and institutional needs. Both entry-level and experienced candidates are encouraged to apply. Please visit this web page ( https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/ ) to learn more about Hollins; its commitment to belonging, pluralism, and student success; faculty development; the university’s students; and the Roanoke Valley. Position and Qualifications Programs offered by the Chemistry Department prepare students to meet the challenges of a complex global society. Facilitated by modern methods of analysis and close working relationships with faculty, students receive theoretical and practical instruction in all major areas of chemistry including analytical chemistry, biochemistry, inorganic chemistry, organic chemistry, and physical chemistry. The department offers B.A. and B.S. degrees in chemistry, including biochemistry concentrations, as well as a B.A. degree in chemistry with a business concentration. The primary teaching need for this position is in the department’s general chemistry sequence. The successful candidate will contribute substantially to introductory chemistry instruction including lecture and laboratory courses serving both majors and non-majors. Depending on departmental needs and the candidate’s qualifications, additional teaching assignments may include courses in other areas of the curriculum. Candidates must have a master’s or Ph.D. degree in chemistry or a master’s or Ph.D. degree in a related field with at least 18 graduate credit hours in chemistry. Additionally, the applicant’s degree must be in hand by July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to student-centered undergraduate teaching and mentoring in a liberal arts environment. Competitive applicants will provide evidence of effective teaching practices, meaningful assessment and feedback, mentoring, and support for student learning and success. Evidence may include prior teaching experience, educational outreach, tutoring, workshops, mentoring of student organizations or research students, formal preparation in teaching and pedagogy, participation in pedagogical professional development, or other relevant experiences. Candidates are encouraged to describe experiences that demonstrate their ability to support student learning, belonging, and success both inside and outside the classroom. Preferred qualifications include the ability to teach organic chemistry or physical chemistry, interest in contributing to the mission of a historically women’s college, and experience working with and mentoring students from a wide variety of backgrounds in the sciences. Application Process Review of applications will begin on Oct. 12, 2026 (extended deadline) and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit the following materials: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position and interest in teaching at a liberal arts institution Statement of teaching philosophy and experience (maximum three pages) that addresses the candidate’s approach to undergraduate teaching and mentoring. Applicants are encouraged to discuss strategies for supporting learning and success in classroom environments where students have various backgrounds, life experiences, and abilities. Curriculum vitae Unofficial graduate transcripts Candidates selected for further consideration will be asked to provide: Sample syllabus from a chemistry course or a course the candidate is prepared to teach Professional letter of recommendation that speaks to the candidate’s ability/experience of mentoring and teaching students emailed directly to chemsearch@hollins.edu by the letter’s author Candidates invited for campus interviews will be asked to provide contact information for two additional professional references. Reference checks will be conducted by the search committee. Applicants should submit materials to chemsearch@hollins.edu . The selected candidate will be asked to submit official transcripts to the Office of the Provost, and once they accept a contingent employment offer, a criminal history background check will be conducted. Additionally, the selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis, and Hollins University will not sponsor employment visas for this role. The salary for this position is $62,500/year. For questions about the position, please contact Dan Derringer, chair of chemistry, at dderringer@hollins.edu . About Hollins Hollins University is dedicated to academic excellence, creativity, belonging, and preparing students for lives of purpose. Hollins provides an outstanding and academically rigorous undergraduate liberal arts education for women and entrepreneurial and innovative graduate programs for all in a gender-inclusive environment. The university promotes opportunities for research, education abroad, and leadership training, and offers real-world experience through global internships supported by an extensive alumnae/i network. Hollins University is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment. No employee or applicant shall be discriminated against on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including transgender status, sexual orientation, pregnancy, and gender identity), national or ethnic origin, age, disability, genetic information, military or veteran status, or any other category protected by applicable state and federal laws. Visit www.hollins.edu for more information."
    },
    "https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/faculty-positions/#english-tenure-track-assistant-or-associate-professor-of-english": {
      "title": "ENGLISH: Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor of English",
      "description": "ENGLISH: Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor of English. (posted 8/3/26) The Department of English and Creative Writing at Hollins University in Roanoke, Virginia invites applications for a tenure-track assistant or associate professor of English. This is a full-time, on-campus position beginning July 1, 2027, and both entry-level and experienced candidates are encouraged to apply. Please visit this web page ( https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/ ) to learn more about Hollins; its commitment to belonging, pluralism, and student success; faculty development; the university’s students; and the Roanoke Valley. Position and Qualifications The Department of English and Creative Writing is seeking a colleague with specialization in Black studies to teach introductory and advanced literature courses and to join a collaborative team with interconnected literature and creative writing programs. The department is one of the university’s largest and is internationally known for its history of supporting developing writers and scholars who go on to shape literary studies. With 10 full-time faculty members, the department offers a B.A. in English & Creative Writing (with an option for student-designed concentrations) and an M.F.A. in Creative Writing. Additionally, the department houses two student-produced literary magazines: Cargoes (all genres) and Gravel (multicultural writing). The holder of this position will teach six courses (or the equivalent) per year, maintain an active research agenda, and perform departmental and institutional service. They will work with undergraduates at all levels from introductory courses to senior honors projects, including writing-intensive courses. They will also contribute to the Hollins core curriculum and provide mentorship and advising to students both within and beyond the English & Creative Writing major. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in English, comparative literature, Black studies, Africana studies, American studies, or related field with specialization in literature in hand by July 1, 2027. The successful candidate for this position will: Demonstrate preparation to teach broadly by defining secondary fields of teaching interest in conjunction with Black studies that will expand curricular offerings. Areas of particular interest for this position include, but are not limited, to LGBTQIA+ literature, genre fiction, poetry studies, and critical writing pedagogy. Demonstrate preparation to mentor and advise a highly engaged student population by narrating their experience or approach to providing support for students from a wide variety of backgrounds. Articulate a clear research agenda that includes plans for peer-reviewed publication. Demonstrate leadership and organizational capabilities that will prepare them to help shape the curriculum at a small, liberal arts university through departmental and institutional service. Preferred candidates will: Be able to teach surveys of African-American literature post-1860 and/or American literature post-1860. Ideal candidates will be able to frame these subjects beyond the constraints of borders. Show interest in public scholarship, creative work, and/or community engagement beyond traditional academic settings. Application Process Screening of candidates will begin on September 14, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit the following materials: A detailed cover letter of no more than three pages A curriculum vitae Candidates advanced to the second stage of review will be invited to submit the following materials: A 1-2 page research statement A one-page statement of teaching philosophy An article-length writing sample Two sample syllabi An unofficial copy of graduate school transcripts Three letters of recommendation (at least one of which must focus specifically on the candidate’s teaching experience and abilities) References will be directly contacted for candidates who proceed to the final interview stage. Applicants should submit materials to englishsearch@hollins.edu . The selected candidate will be asked to submit official transcripts to the Office of the Provost, and once they accept a contingent employment offer, a criminal history background check will be conducted. Additionally, the selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis, and Hollins University will not sponsor employment visas for this role. The salary for this position is $62,500/year if the selected candidate is hired as an assistant professor and is $69,000/year if the selected candidate is hired as an associate professor. For questions about the position, please contact search chair Michelle De Groot at degrootmc@hollins.edu . About Hollins Hollins University is dedicated to academic excellence, creativity, belonging, and preparing students for lives of purpose. Hollins provides an outstanding and academically rigorous undergraduate liberal arts education for women and entrepreneurial and innovative graduate programs for all in a gender-inclusive environment. The university promotes opportunities for research, education abroad, and leadership training, and offers real-world experience through global internships supported by an extensive alumnae/i network. Hollins University is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment. No employee or applicant shall be discriminated against on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including transgender status, sexual orientation, pregnancy, and gender identity), national or ethnic origin, age, disability, genetic information, military or veteran status, or any other category protected by applicable state and federal laws. Visit www.hollins.edu for more information."
    },
    "https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/faculty-positions/#environmental-public-health-tenure-track-assistant-or-associate-professor-of-environmental-public-health": {
      "title": "ENVIRONMENTAL PUBLIC HEALTH: Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor of Environmental Public Health",
      "description": "ENVIRONMENTAL PUBLIC HEALTH: Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor of Environmental Public Health. (posted 8/3/26) The Department of Applied Social Sciences at Hollins University in Roanoke, Virginia invites applications for a tenure-track assistant or associate professor of environmental public health. This is a full-time, on-campus position beginning July 1, 2027, and both entry-level and experienced candidates are encouraged to apply. Please visit this web page ( https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/ ) to learn more about Hollins; its commitment to belonging, pluralism, and student success; faculty development; the university’s students; and the Roanoke Valley. Position and Qualifications The Bachelor of Science in environmental public health is a proposed new major within the public health program set to launch in fall 2028. Public health is an interdisciplinary program that offers undergraduates a well-rounded education integrating the social and natural sciences to champion health equity within population health. The program highly values experiential learning, and students regularly engage in health-related activism, internships, study-abroad, and other experiential-learning experiences. A key responsibility for the position will be to develop the curriculum and envision the design of the environmental public health major with support from colleagues across public health, biology, environmental science/studies, and chemistry. The position carries a teaching load equivalent to six courses per year. The successful candidate will be committed to teaching excellence at a liberal arts institution. They will also have the opportunity to develop upper-level courses in their area of specialization and will teach in the core curriculum (Hollins’ general education program). Additional duties include service to the department and university, mentorship, academic advising, supervision of undergraduate capstone projects and internships, and faculty governance. Candidates for this position will have a doctoral degree (Ph.D., Dr.PH., or equivalent) in environmental health, occupational health and safety, industrial hygiene, public health, or closely related fields like chemistry, biology, or environmental science, in hand by July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will be able to teach introduction to environmental health, environmental health hazards, toxicology, risk management, and air or water quality. Candidates are expected to maintain an active research agenda in one of the aforementioned areas that engages undergraduates within the framework of a small, teaching-focused, liberal arts institution, along with a willingness to work with a variety of undergraduate students in pursuit of their career goals. Preferred candidates will have demonstrated experience in developing relationships and establishing external collaborations with industrial and community partners. Application Process Full consideration will be given to applicants submitting materials by September 4, 2026. To apply, please submit the following materials: A cover letter addressing teaching philosophy and experiences and abilities in teaching environmental public health (no more than 2 pages) A curriculum vitae Candidates advanced to the second stage of review will be invited to submit the following materials: Statement that envisions how they would approach setting and achieving program goals for students through curriculum design in the first year, including potential career trajectories for graduates (no more than 2 pages) A research statement (no more than 2 pages) plus required equipment and resource list An unofficial copy of graduate school transcripts Candidates who proceed to the final interview stage will be asked to provide contact information for three references, at least one of whom can speak to the candidate’s teaching experience and abilities. Applicants should submit materials to ephsearch@hollins.edu . The selected candidate will be asked to submit official transcripts to the Office of the Provost, and once they accept a contingent employment offer, a criminal history background check will be conducted. Additionally, the selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis, and Hollins University will not sponsor employment visas for this role. The salary for this position is $62,500/year if the selected candidate is hired as an assistant professor and is $69,000/year if the selected candidate is hired as an associate professor. For questions about the position, please contact search chair Susan Eagle, Assistant Professor of Public Health, at eaglese@hollins.edu . About Hollins Hollins University is dedicated to academic excellence, creativity, belonging, and preparing students for lives of purpose. Hollins provides an outstanding and academically rigorous undergraduate liberal arts education for women and entrepreneurial and innovative graduate programs for all in a gender-inclusive environment. The university promotes opportunities for research, education abroad, and leadership training, and offers real-world experience through global internships supported by an extensive alumnae/i network. Hollins University is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment. No employee or applicant shall be discriminated against on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including transgender status, sexual orientation, pregnancy, and gender identity), national or ethnic origin, age, disability, genetic information, military or veteran status, or any other category protected by applicable state and federal laws. Visit www.hollins.edu for more information."
    },
    "https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/faculty-positions/#history-tenure-track-assistant-professor-of-history-for-twentieth-century-europe-and-the-world": {
      "title": "HISTORY: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of History for Twentieth-Century Europe and the World",
      "description": "HISTORY: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of History for Twentieth-Century Europe and the World. (posted 8/3/26) The history program at Hollins University in Roanoke, Virginia invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor of history for twentieth-century Europe and the world. This is a full-time, on-campus position beginning July 1, 2027, and both entry-level and experienced candidates are encouraged to apply. Please visit this web page ( https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/ ) to learn more about Hollins; its commitment to belonging, pluralism, and student success; faculty development; the university’s students; and the Roanoke Valley. About the History Program The history program at Hollins empowers students with critical reading and analytical writing skills through engaging courses aimed at providing all students with the opportunity to discover themselves and the world around them through knowledge of history. History is one of the top majors at Hollins, and students have a strong track record of earning research and writing awards; competitive national fellowships and scholarships; admission to top graduate programs; and employment in history-related fields including teaching, librarianship, and museum education. History faculty contribute to the university’s general education curriculum and to interdisciplinary programs such as Gender and Women’s Studies, International Studies, Environmental Studies, and Public Health. With a small number of faculty, there has been significant progress in recent years in diversifying the curriculum by adding courses in areas such as global slavery, poverty, transgender and queer history, and the history of witchcraft. Role Responsibilities Teach six courses/year Offer a variety of history courses on modern Europe and its global interconnections, from the introductory to advanced level, including the Modern Europe survey Contribute to the university’s general education program, the Core (https://www.hollins.edu/academics/core-curriculum/) Perform service at the departmental and university level Mentor and advise students Position Qualifications The successful candidate will have: Ph.D. in history in hand by July 1, 2027 An active research agenda focused in twentieth-century Europe and its global interconnections Preparation to teach courses from the introductory to advanced level on modern Europe and its global interconnections Demonstrated commitment to creating learning environments that support every student The preferred candidate will have: Research or teaching experience in one or more of the following: environmental history, history of empire and decolonization, or public history Ability to teach a history course focused on one or more of the following: modern Africa, modern Latin America, or modern Pacific or Indian Ocean worlds Application Process Preference will be given to candidates who apply by September 11, 2026 when screening of applications will begin. To apply, please submit the following materials: A cover letter that addresses the candidate’s research agenda and teaching experience and interests and demonstrates how the candidate meets the required and any preferred qualifications A curriculum vitae Candidates advanced to the second stage of review will be invited to submit the following materials: A statement that explains the candidate’s teaching philosophy and their commitment to creating a learning environment that supports every student A writing sample of no more than 30 pages that demonstrates the candidate’s research (e.g., a published or in-progress article or book chapter, a conference paper, or a dissertation chapter) A sample syllabus An unofficial copy of graduate school transcripts Three letters of recommendation, at least one of which can speak to the candidate’s teaching experience and at least one of which can speak to the candidate’s research agenda Candidates who proceed to the final interview stage will be asked for contact information for three references. Applicants should submit materials to historysearch@hollins.edu . The selected candidate will be asked to submit official transcripts to the Office of the Provost, and once they accept a contingent employment offer, a criminal history background check will be conducted. Additionally, the selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis, and Hollins University will not sponsor employment visas for this role. The salary for this position is $62,500/year. For questions about the position, please contact search chair Dr. Rachel Nuñez at nunezrm@hollins.edu . About Hollins Hollins University is dedicated to academic excellence, creativity, belonging, and preparing students for lives of purpose. Hollins provides an outstanding and academically rigorous undergraduate liberal arts education for women and entrepreneurial and innovative graduate programs for all in a gender-inclusive environment. The university promotes opportunities for research, education abroad, and leadership training, and offers real-world experience through global internships supported by an extensive alumnae/i network. Hollins University is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment. No employee or applicant shall be discriminated against on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including transgender status, sexual orientation, pregnancy, and gender identity), national or ethnic origin, age, disability, genetic information, military or veteran status, or any other category protected by applicable state and federal laws. Visit www.hollins.edu for more information."
    },
    "https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/faculty-positions/#sociology-tenure-track-assistant-professor-of-sociology": {
      "title": "SOCIOLOGY: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Sociology",
      "description": "SOCIOLOGY: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Sociology. (posted 8/3/26) The Department of Applied Social Sciences at Hollins University in Roanoke, Virginia invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor of sociology with a preferred focus on criminology and/or digital sociology. This is a full-time, on-campus position beginning July 1, 2027, and both entry-level and experienced candidates are encouraged to apply. Please visit this web page ( https://www.hollins.edu/about-hollins/jobs/ ) to learn more about Hollins; its commitment to belonging, pluralism, and student success; faculty development; the university’s students; and the Roanoke Valley. Position and Qualifications Positioned in the Department of Applied Social Sciences, the sociology program at Hollins serves undergraduate students from a wide range of interests and academic and professional goals. The program focuses on building robust skillsets that allow students to pursue their areas of interest with theoretical and methodological rigor. The sociology faculty work collaboratively with other university programs like public health, political science, economics, and communication studies to support students across the social sciences disciplines. Department faculty are committed to supporting students through intentional mentoring and advising as they pursue meaningful sociological pathways to academic and/or industry careers. Current students in the sociology program are driven to develop practical skillsets with which they can apply their sociological knowledge to a variety of important social issues with criminology as a key area of study. Students from the major have pursued diverse pathways from Hollins that have included plans for law school, graduate school in sociology and criminology, and professional programs and career paths. This position carries a teaching load equivalent to six courses per academic year. The faculty member must be prepared to teach introductory and advanced courses within sociology as well as courses anchored in the core curriculum designed to serve all majors. This position also carries the potential to contribute to the expansion of undergraduate courses and research opportunities in sociology. Additional duties include maintaining an active research agenda, service to the department and university, mentoring and advising undergraduate students, and curricular development. The successful candidate for this position will be broadly trained in sociological theory and research methods. They will be able to contribute to the core curriculum in sociology and support methodological course offerings across the social sciences, such as introduction to sociology, social problems, social theory, research methods, and qualitative or quantitative methods, in addition to substantive elective classes. A sociologist is sought who has a commitment to mentoring students from a wide variety of backgrounds through their sociological education and development as social scientists. The preferred candidate will also have a commitment to research and teaching in areas of criminology and/or digital sociology. Experience preparing students for career success in the social sciences and working in a collaborative or interdisciplinary environment are also highly valuable to the department. Competitive candidates will also demonstrate their commitment to teaching in a liberal arts context and ability to contribute to core curriculum offerings at the undergraduate level, in addition to a dedication to mentoring upper-level students through research, capstone projects, and experiential learning requirements. ABD candidates are welcome to apply, but all applicants must have a Ph.D. in sociology in hand by July 1, 2027. Application Process Screening of candidates will begin on September 14, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit, in a single PDF, the following materials: A cover letter A curriculum vitae A statement of teaching philosophy and experience of no more than two pages Candidates advanced to the second stage of review will be invited to submit the following materials: A research statement of no more than two pages A writing sample – chapter or article length A sample syllabus An unofficial copy of graduate school transcripts Candidates who proceed to the final interview stage will be asked for three letters of recommendation (at least one of which must focus specifically on the candidate’s teaching experience and abilities) and associated contact information for those references. Applicants should submit materials to sociologysearch@hollins.edu . The selected candidate will be asked to submit official transcripts to the Office of the Provost, and once they accept a contingent employment offer, a criminal history background check will be conducted. Additionally, the selected candidate must be authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis, and Hollins University will not sponsor employment visas for this role. The salary for this position is $62,500/year. For questions about the position, please contact search chair Sadie Snow at snowse@hollins.edu . About Hollins Hollins University is dedicated to academic excellence, creativity, belonging, and preparing students for lives of purpose. Hollins provides an outstanding and academically rigorous undergraduate liberal arts education for women and entrepreneurial and innovative graduate programs for all in a gender-inclusive environment. The university promotes opportunities for research, education abroad, and leadership training, and offers real-world experience through global internships supported by an extensive alumnae/i network. Hollins University is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment. No employee or applicant shall be discriminated against on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including transgender status, sexual orientation, pregnancy, and gender identity), national or ethnic origin, age, disability, genetic information, military or veteran status, or any other category protected by applicable state and federal laws. Visit www.hollins.edu for more information."
    }
